==Old Norse==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|non|it}} {{q|dual form}}, {{l|non|ér}} {{q|plural form}}
* {{l|non|-du}}, {{l|non|-ðu}}, {{l|non|-tu}} {{q|enclitic forms}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|non|gem-pro|*þū}}, from {{inh|non|ine-pro|*túh₂}}. Cognates: see there.

===Pronoun===
{{head|non|pronoun|cat2=personal pronouns}}

# {{lb|non|in the singular}} [[you]] ([[thou]])
#* Hávamál 112.
#*: Ráðum'k '''þér''', Loddfáfnir, 
#*: at '''þú''' ráð nemir, 
#*: njóta mun'''du''' ef '''þú''' nemr, 
#*: '''þér''' munu góð ef '''þú''' getr: 
#*: Nótt '''þú''' rís-at 
#*: nema á njósn sér 
#*: eða '''þú''' leitir '''þér''' innan út staðar.

====Usage notes====
It may become enclitic when after a verb, that is, be appended to the preceding word as either {{m|non|-du}}, {{m|non|-ðu}} or {{m|non|-tu}} depending on the consonant cluster. The rules for this are the same as for the past tense dental suffixes of the class 1 weak verbs. This may happen more often in Old Norse, as the personal pronoun is often used with the singular imperative. This is not to say, however, that whenever '''þú''' comes after a verb, it will always take an enclitic form. It could well stay separate for the sake of emphasis. With the clitics, this is how it ends up looking. 
* {{suffixusex|non|fórt|fórtu}}
* {{suffixusex|non|kenn|kenndu}}
* {{suffixusex|non|far|farðu}}
* {{suffixusex|non|miskunna|miskunnaðu}}
Note, as seen in the last two examples, that this does not cause u-umlaut.

